                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238058989",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198030326907",
                            "num_reviews": 218,
                            "playtime_forever": 417,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 417,
                            "playtime_at_review": 417,
                            "last_played": 1792281159
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Imagine a [i]Hitman[/i] game taking place in a single map, with all your targets in that one map, you're only allowed to take down your targets with random items you find throughout the [b][u]entire[/u][/b] map in one [b]very[/b] specific way and you're given one of those targets to kill each level at random. \n\nYou can find [b]any[/b] items related to [b]any[/b] of your targets at [b]any[/b] given time and place, only straightforward information you're given is your target's location at the beginning of each level and the game treats you like you're some [i]NASA[/i]-level quantum computer specialized in rube goldberg machines, being able to identify every kind of [i]Final Destination[/i]-esque way to kill any human being under any situation via your god-like algorythm.\n\nOther than giving you your target's location on your map, you also have 3 more ways to get hints about your intended way of taking down your target for that level; a --sometimes uncomfortably so-- vague riddle once per-level, some lines of dialgoue in the cutscenes or some stuff NPCs keep repeating at you and a couple of observational notes your character writes down on his notebook at certain times. Those \"observations\" can vary from [i]\"I hate Mr. A, I must find a way to kill him!\"[/i] to [i]\"Finally, Mr. A is alone in the room, now I have to get creative.\"[/i], so these \"hints\" being helpful in any way was clearly an afterthought.\n\nThere are [i]some[/i] slightly redeeming qualities with the game like the unintentionally hilarious, [i]CoD[/i] end-of-the-game killcam style cutscenes that play after you finally combine everything you have to successfully off your target or [i]b-movie[/i] quality voice acting performances but the keyword here is [u]unintentional[/u]. It might not be a terrible game per-se, but a way too clunky and frustrating one to the point of forcing you too look for enjoyment within the game, through aspects developers didn't mean you to find it. Truly a shame, because the overall concept had so, so much potential.\n\nI played it because of nostalgia --thanks to having fond memories of watching a blonde Swede playing it 13 years ago-- and since it was seasonally appropiate. Can't really recommend if the concept alone isn't making you reach for your wallet.",
